1
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:15,800
You may be asking yourself,

2
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,000
why do I need specific training

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,360
on using methylene chloride in my laboratory?

4
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:23,760
Well, that's a fair question.

5
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,720
The answer to that question is that long-term

6
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:30,420
or repeated high exposure levels to methylene chloride

7
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:33,360
can result in significant adverse health effects

8
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,240
on the heart, liver, and brain,

9
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:40,880
and may include an increased risk of developing cancer.

10
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:43,480
The nature of the exposure effects

11
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:47,560
prompted OSHA to release the methylene chloride standard,

12
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:51,520
29 CFR 1910.1052.

13
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:55,100
OSHA built this standard specifically

14
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:57,220
because methylene chloride's hazards

15
00:00:57,220 --> 00:01:00,100
and how it was being used in industry.

16
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:04,820
Laboratory use requires smaller volumes,

17
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:07,780
but the OSHA regulation still applies

18
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:11,100
and it is prudent to pay attention to the hazards

19
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,180
and ensure effective control measures

20
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:14,300
are being used.
